items.body:
## What this changes

Ling-3.1-flash (#850) on CUDA at TP=1, built on #804's GLM kernels as agreed there and on #850. #804 is in main since 1.0.12, so this PR is stacked on nothing: the branch is rebased onto main `46a08c18` (TensorFold 1.0.12), and every commit on it is this PR's. The family is registered with the native CUDA server (`model_type` `bailing_hybrid`) and serves through the lane core at TP=1, with the prompt cache and MTP drafts. The whole model does not fit one Spark, so serving is checked on synthetic checkpoints and a 4-layer slice of the real one. `docs/recipes/ling-3.1.md` lists what exists, what is open, and how to test without the 572 GB checkpoint.

- **#804's kernels, extended, with GLM's bytes unchanged:**
  - group-limited top-k (8 groups, keep 4) and a per-layer shared SwiGLU limit (`f9fc1a78`); a division-free branch for groups that are whole lane columns, which GLM's single group never enters (`e78d23bb`);
  - GLM's `swiglu` keeps its limit as given, 0 included, as at `6380c8da`; only Ling's `swigluWith` maps 0 to unclamped (`32055e9e`, `991383f3`);
  - MLA with Ling's 64 roped key columns, 128-wide heads and a head-wise gate (`7d184cb7`);
  - KDA checked as is on Ling's layer 0 (`ef8f12f3`). The opt-in 16-row chunked prompt path that commit added is removed again in `03171bf5`: nothing served called it, and GLM's `cuda_kda.zig` is `6380c8da`'s file again;
  - Ling's SwiGLU clamp order, `min(silu(g), L) * clip(u, -L, L)`, as new kernel instances (`bfb44ddc`);
  - `tf_pack_fp8_experts` in `modelopt_pack.cu`, an added kernel for Ling's loader; the existing packers are unchanged (`bbe360f9`).
- **`zig/src/families/ling/`, format-agnostic:**
  - config and weight map (`a6559571`);
  - a loader that takes each tensor's format from the checkpoint, bf16, block FP8 or ModelOpt NVFP4, through `qlinear` and `experts`. EXL3 is refused and points to #832 (`d46e3723`);
  - `ling-synth` for small synthetic checkpoints in each format;
  - the TP=1 layer graph with prompt rows, decode windows and `commit(keep)` (`6f583baf`);
  - serving (`a3f14d30`): `Engine` with a sequence per slot, prompt chunks cut at any row, verify windows of up to 16 rows, shared windows of up to 8 streams, greedy and sampled draws; `Lanes`, GLM's `cuda_lanes.zig` without the ranks; `native.zig` with the server hooks and the prompt cache's `snap_*` hooks. A kept state is the sequence's arena copied whole, and a mark may fall on any row;
  - the MTP layer (`model.layers.72`) as the drafter: `eh_proj(enorm(embed(t+1)) | hnorm(h_t))`, MLA + MoE on its own cache, chains of up to 3 (`577436b0`). The server loads it unless `--no-drafts`;
  - routed experts staged in batches through pinned memory and packed on the GPU (`bbe360f9`);
  - a speed pass on the copies and small FP8 expert runs, same bytes (`99652bab`, `b3faea69`, `5b006370`), and timing tests (`7bd7a40a`, `412f66fc`, `0ffce4f7`, `bd7c23e3`);
  - the recipe (`faf7af1c`).
- **Review fixes** (one commit each, each with a regression test that fails with the fix reverted):
  - config refuses every geometry the kernels don't take, at load, with a named error: `KdaGeometry`, `MlaGeometry`, `Width`, `TiedHead` (`66579cba`);
  - `Store.alloc` owns a buffer only once it is in both lists; `fake_driver.zig` fails every driver call in turn (`674163fa`);
  - rows past a sequence's capacity are refused before any layer runs (`82b3817b`);
  - routing groups of one and a single pick are refused at load, as HF's `group_limited_topk` can't run them (`92afed61`);
  - `Engine.init` frees sequence 0 and the slot list when `bind(0)` or the last sync fails (`0ba2adfb`);
  - a new stream's slot is chosen before its map entry exists (`0ba510ad`);
  - every tensor of the full checkpoint checked against the loader's names, dtypes and shapes, from shard headers alone, through one shape table, `names.dims` (`5649c63b`);
  - the oracles check their HF source against `inclusionAI/Ling-3.1-flash@a6943f4b` by sha256 (`e4c9bcc5`);
  - a segment the scratch cannot hold is refused before any layer runs, also on the MTP layer (`5e931c3a`);
  - staging and loaded weights are freed only after the stream drains; a failed drain leaks them on purpose (`1395e267`).

## Receipt

- Environment: TensorFold `46a08c18` (1.0.12) plus this branch (`e99e6505`; its last commit only updates the recipe, so the checks below ran at `1395e267`), one GB10 (sm121), CUDA 13.0, Zig 0.17.0. Checkpoint: `inclusionAI/Ling-3.1-flash`, block FP8, pinned at `a6943f4b`; its real layers read one at a time.
- Serving (`ling-lanes`, under upstream's round loop, prompts in 37-row chunks), bf16, FP8 and NVFP4, each with and without `--mtp`: 100 and 109 checks pass in each format. Tokens in every check, state bytes in the engine-level ones:
  - chunked == whole, at chunks of 512, 37 and 16 rows;
  - drafted == plain, greedy and sampled, windows of 3 to 16 rows cut anywhere; a 9-row window kept to 4 then a 5-row window equals 9 single rows;
  - resumed == fresh, at marks 37, 101, 150, 263 and 349 (not multiples of 16);
  - concurrent == solo, nine cases of three streams, and shared windows of 2 streams equal lone twins;
  - MTP-drafted == plain, greedy, sampled, resumed and concurrent; chained drafts equal ones computed by hand;
  - the reply equals the serial forward's argmax at all 160 of 160 rows;
  - a refused request (past the sequence's capacity, or past the scratch's) leaves the arenas byte for byte as they were, and the slot then serves as a fresh one does.

  Served logits against `ling_forward.py`'s float64 on the same picks: 2.09e-2 to 2.24e-2 rel L2, as far as the HF model's own bf16 rounding points are (2.10e-2 to 2.25e-2). On the real FP8 release, `--layers 0,1,2,3` (real embedding, layers 0-3, final norm and head) passes the same exactness checks; that run is not in the re-run at this head.
- Graph exactness, bit for bit, in bf16, FP8 and NVFP4 (`ling-forward-synth`): a prompt cut at 48 equals the uncut prompt; an 8-row verify window equals 8 decode rows; keeping 3 of 8 rows and then running 5 more leaves every KDA state, conv window and MLA cache row equal to the serial run; two sequences batched equal each run alone. On real layers, a prompt cut at 300 equals the uncut prompt and a 16-row window equals 16 decode rows.
- Against the reference:
  - real FP8 layers 0, 4, 7, 65 and 71 against the HF bf16 model's captured chain: rel L2 3.1e-3 to 3.8e-3, excluding the 0 to 21 of 512 rows whose top-8 picks flip on a near tie. Layer 4 at this head: 3.126e-3, its output bytes equal to `a78a7a49`'s;
  - MLA layer 7 alone: 4.4e-3 from HF's bf16 output (HF's bf16 output is 3.6e-3 from float64);
  - KDA layer 0: 3.591e-3 from fla, within the float64 bound;
  - the MTP layer (`ling-mtp-real`) against HF's `BailingMoeV3MTPLayer`: 7.28e-3 on the 488 of 511 rows whose top-8 agrees;
  - routing on layer 4: the same experts in the same order on all 512 rows;
  - synthetic forwards: within the float64 bound, as far from float64 as the bf16 model's own rounding is.
- Full checkpoint (`ling-ckpt-check`, shard headers only): 54 shards, 214,025 tensors (3,100 of the MTP layer), 573,753,281,152 bytes, no name, dtype or shape mismatch, nothing missing or extra. The shard data is not hashed.
- Fault paths (`ling-init-faults`, on `fake_driver.zig`): every driver call and host allocation of the open and a kept state fails in turn with transfers pending, then every call from N on. Over bf16, FP8 and NVFP4, with and without MTP: 15,180 failure points (1,804 failing with a transfer pending), none leaves anything held, leaked, freed twice or freed before a drain.
- Loading one MoE layer's 512 FP8 experts (7.5 GiB): 6.1 s from local disk with the GPU packer, 14.3 s with the host packers before; host peak 1.3 GiB. Every packed byte equals the host packers' (`ling-pack`).
- Decode and prompt speed: not measured. The whole model does not run on one Spark. The speed pass is measured per op only: prompt KDA and MLP column copies -50 % (`99652bab`); FP8 expert time -7 to -27 % for prompt chunks up to 768 rows (`5b006370`).
- GLM unchanged:
  - `glm-route-old` (select, and swiglu at limits 10 and 0) and `glm-mla-same` (13 launches, 14.3 MB of outputs) run #804's `6380c8da` fatbins next to the new ones, byte for byte; `ling-route-old` checks the division-free branch against the older select's bytes;
  - every kernel that existed before keeps its SASS and resource usage (`cuobjdump`, all fatbins);
  - `ling-clamp-old` checks the kept expert instances' bytes;
  - @Bizuayeu ran GLM's checks on the GLM-5.3 NVFP4 checkpoint on three GB10 hosts at `c48be499`, `ac58df63` and `e29e0c20` (now `03171bf5`): one host, TP=2 and TP=3, and the server at TP=2 against `6380c8da`'s replies. All pass. GLM's and the shared code have not changed since `03171bf5`.
- Tests run at `1395e267`: `glm-ref`, `glm-route-old`, `ling-route-old`, `glm-mla-same`, `glm-kda-ling`, `ling-slots`, `ling-ckpt-check` (three synthetic checkpoints and the full release), `ling-init-faults` and `ling-lanes` (three formats, with and without `--mtp`; ids and picks byte-equal to `a78a7a49`), `ling-weights` (three formats, worst 0.433 of the bound), `ling-pack` layer 4, `ling-layer-real` layer 4; `zig build test` (1390/1398, 8 skipped), `test-ling` 30/31 (1 skipped: it needs `TF_LING_INDEX`), `test-cuda-host` 237/237, `zig fmt --check`, `lean_check` 517, as on main. Deliberate mutations, all caught: routing 6, MLA 6, config 8, loader 5, graph 6, clamp 5, serving 5, MTP 5, and each review fix reverted alone.
- Not run, and why:
  - a full 72-layer Ling forward, the real model served end to end, and MTP acceptance on real text (572 GB in FP8 needs five Sparks and TP);
  - TP=2 for Ling (one Spark);
  - Metal.

## What is still open

- TP>1. The loader and engine are TP=1; cuts would follow `glm/cuda_shard.zig` and #855.
- EXL3, through #832. It is refused at load, naming #832.
- The recorder. #848 is in main, but Ling gives no tapped rows yet, so `--record` is refused for `bailing_hybrid`. The rows are the final-norm rows `Engine.verify` already keeps.
- MTP acceptance on real text, once a whole model runs.
- bf16 routed experts run every expert on every row (no grouped bf16 kernel): correct, for tests, not for speed.
- Fused same-format projections (q|k|v, gate|up) without column copies.
- A real NVFP4 checkpoint. Only synthetic NVFP4 checkpoints have been loaded.

## Checklist

- [x] Every token still goes through the lane rounds. No serial path, and nothing that needs drafts off.
- [x] Drafted output equals `"draft": false`, a resumed prompt equals a fresh one, and concurrent equals solo. (On synthetic checkpoints in three formats and the real model's first 4 layers; not on the full model.)
- [x] No precision traded for speed. If the bits change, the description says which sums change and why.
- [ ] Prompt processing is no slower than the last release. (Not measured for Ling; GLM's kernels are unchanged.)
- [x] The description says which platforms I ran, Metal M1 to M5 and CUDA, and which I could not.
- [x] New tests fail before the change, pass after it, and skip cleanly without their dependency.
- [x] Comments and docstrings are one line. No measurements or history in the source.
- [x] No personal data, machine names, internal hosts or local paths. No AI attribution lines.

**Open question for @ashhart (from #850 and your reply of 2026-10-15):** group-limited top-k is built into #804's `tf_glm_topk`, since Ling's MoE runs through #804's routing. It ranks sigmoid(logit)+bias as HF does for Ling; #798's `moe_glue.cu` ranks logit+bias, so the two can't simply replace each other. Where should the shared routing live?
